WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.680
INTRO:
Eversince the fed started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:06.960
to print boatloads of money, people have been&nbsp;
concerned about the potential of high inflation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.440
For quite some time, Jerome Powell dismissed&nbsp;
these concerns claiming that if there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:24.320
elavated inflation readings, it would be&nbsp;
transitory. Despite his confidence, many were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.680
still skeptical about inflation being transitory.&nbsp;
I mean, basic logic would tell you that increasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:36.000
the money supply by $6.4 trillion would have&nbsp;
some sort of economic consequence. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.600
Jerome continued to claim that inflation would be&nbsp;
transitory meeting after meeting after meeting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.080
After a meeting with Joe Biden near the end of&nbsp;
last year, however, Jerome would basically turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.640
on a dime. Suddenly, Jerome thought that the word&nbsp;
“transitory” should be retired when it came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:01:15.360
describing inflation. Aside from admitting&nbsp;
that inflation had gotten way out of hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:21.040
the entire fed became extremely hawkish. They went&nbsp;
from raising interest rates 0.25% per meeting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:26.880
0.5% per meeting to 0.75% per meeting. Now,&nbsp;
there’s even rumors that the fed will raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.120
interest rates by a full percentage point in&nbsp;
one meeting. Despite all of their efforts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.840
we have yet to see inflation reading peak in a&nbsp;
meaningful way. In fact, the latest CPI reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:42.560
put year over year inflation at 9.1%, and it&nbsp;
seems like 10% could be just around the corner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.280
So, whatever happened to “transitory” inflation&nbsp;
and how did the fed get inflation so wrong?

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.720
TRANSITORY INFLATION EXPLAINED:&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.920
Before we get into why the fed&nbsp;
thought inflation would be transitory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.520
I think it’s important to discuss what&nbsp;
exactly they meant by transitory inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.720
in the first place. Many often people associate&nbsp;
transitory inflation with transitory price hikes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.280
but this is not what transitory inflation means.&nbsp;
Transitory inflation simply means that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.720
inflation reading itself will come down to normal&nbsp;
levels of 2-3%. In such a scenario, the price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.120
hikes would still be permanent. It’s just that&nbsp;
the time period in which prices were being hiked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:24.800
wouldn’t last that long. For prices to actually go&nbsp;
down, inflation would not only have to come down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.000
but it would have to go negative and we would have&nbsp;
to see deflation. This is obviously not something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.560
that the fed promised. So, hopefully that cleared&nbsp;
up the confusion between transitory inflation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.280
transtiory price hikes. But, why did the fed even&nbsp;
think that inflation would be transitory in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:45.120
first place. They literally just increased&nbsp;
the money by 33% within a matter of 2 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.080
Well, one of the reasons the fed believed&nbsp;
inflation would be transitory is surging demand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.120
You see, the first time we saw elevated inflation&nbsp;
readings was in the second quarter of 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.200
This was shortly after vaccines started to&nbsp;
become widely available throughout America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.360
So, the theory was that economic participation&nbsp;
rose significantly throughout this time period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.280
as people started to freely go to&nbsp;
restaurants, bars, hotels, vacations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:13.040
shopping, etc. This unprecedented influx of demand&nbsp;
would of course allow companies to raise prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.280
But, overtime, it was expected that people would&nbsp;
pull back spending to normal levels which should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.720
theoretically ease demand and allow prices to&nbsp;
stabilize. Aside from this surge in demand, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.320
labor market was still rather weak. Throughout the&nbsp;
first half of 2021, we primarily saw unemployment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:34.240
figures in the 5 to 6% range. As you can see, this&nbsp;
is a rather high reading on a historical basis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.000
So, even though some people were getting&nbsp;
their vaccines and spending like crazy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.800
a good portion of America was still struggling&nbsp;
to get a job and make ends meet. These guys were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.280
obviously not going to be spending a bunch of&nbsp;
money, so it was expected that these folks would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.920
continue to weigh down demand which should keep&nbsp;
inflation in check. The problem with this theory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.120
however, was that the labor market would recover&nbsp;
faster than anyone expected. Usually, it takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:03.360
several years for unemployment to drop from 6% to&nbsp;
3%, but this time it only took a matter of months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.160
So, not only were people spending more money,&nbsp;
but the labor market was stronger than ever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.480
On top of these theoretical reasons, I suspect&nbsp;
recency bias also played a role in the feds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:18.080
perspective. Eversince the 2008 financial&nbsp;
crisis, inflation has been extraordinarily low.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:23.680
In fact, throughout much of the 2010s, the fed&nbsp;
had to fight to prop up inflation to 2% because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:30.240
kept dropping towards 0. Not to mention, it’s been&nbsp;
nearly 50 years since we saw high inflation, so it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.480
wouldn’t be that surprising to hear that the Fed’s&nbsp;
perspective was skewed by recent economic history.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.880
WHY THE FED WAS WRONG:&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.040
Alright, so we’ve discussed some of the reasons&nbsp;
that the fed believed high inflation would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:48.880
transitory, but why did they end up being wrong?&nbsp;
Well, there were a lot of factors that were out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.360
their control and they simply didn’t pay enough&nbsp;
attention to these factors such as the supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.520
shortage. The fed along with business leaders&nbsp;
have been discussing the supply shortage since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.960
the beginning, but none of them really expected&nbsp;
things to get this bad. The overwhelming consensus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:07.520
was that supply chains were simply having a bit of&nbsp;
trouble getting restarted. A lot of manufacturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.840
plants had to rehire employees, implement new&nbsp;
covid protocol, work around lockdowns, and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.720
on and so forth. So, it wasn’t that surprising&nbsp;
that we were seeing supply chain bottlenecks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.760
As things eased back to normal, it was expected&nbsp;
that these issues would just sort themselves out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:26.400
but we’re actually seeing the exact opposite&nbsp;
take place. The supply shortage is worse than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.320
ever and economists are predicting that&nbsp;
this could easily last for another year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.560
Even Apple who has one of the most established&nbsp;
supply chains in the world was predicting sales to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.920
dive by $8 billion due to shortages and lockdowns.&nbsp;
So, not only were we seeing rapidly recovering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:44.320
demand and labor market, but we were seeing an&nbsp;
unprecedented shortage in supply. And this turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:49.520
out to be a deadly combination. Supply wasn’t&nbsp;
the only area in which we saw shortages either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:53.840
Another major shortage we’ve been facing is the&nbsp;
labor shortage. The mass layoffs that came at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.840
the beginning of the pandemic caused many to&nbsp;
learn new skills, look for higher paying jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.320
and make themselves more valuable. This has made&nbsp;
it extremely difficult for companies to find low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:08.560
level labor. Take Disney for example. They have&nbsp;
33,000 less employees today than they did in 2019.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.040
A similar situation can be seen at Starbucks&nbsp;
as well. As these companies struggle to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:17.120
enough labor, they’re being forced to pay&nbsp;
higher salaries. And while this is great in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:21.600
the short term, this eventually gets passed&nbsp;
onto customers leading to higher inflation.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.840
THE FED’S MASTER PLAN:&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:29.360
So far, we’ve covered the surface level&nbsp;
reasoning behind “transitory” inflation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:33.680
The fed though inflation would be transitory&nbsp;
due to A and B but they were wrong because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:39.920
C and D. This is the overwhelming narrative from&nbsp;
the mainstream media and even the fed itself. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.040
what if they weren’t wrong. What if they knew&nbsp;
that inflation would be high for quite some time?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:50.480
Now, this may sound like a conspiracy theory, but&nbsp;
hear me out. Even if the Fed knew that inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:54.880
would be high, do you really think they would just&nbsp;
come out and say it? That would simply cause mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.640
panic and the Fed wants to keep markets and people&nbsp;
as calm and level headed as possible. So, it makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.600
sense that they would try to underplay how bad&nbsp;
inflation would be. Also, I know a lot of you guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:10.080
think that the people at the fed are idiots and&nbsp;
that they have no idea what they’re doing. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:15.040
do you really think they would’ve missed something&nbsp;
so big? I mean, literally half of America was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:19.760
calling for high inflation. If a good portion of&nbsp;
everyday people could see high inflation coming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:24.880
I suspect fed members with decades of experience&nbsp;
could also see it coming. But then, why did the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:30.000
fed not take any action? Well, the thing to keep&nbsp;
in mind is that the fed has two roles which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.440
to maintain low inflation and to maintain low&nbsp;
unemployment. Throughout 2020 and much of 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:39.200
the need to boost employment was simply a&nbsp;
higher priority for them than trying to minimize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:43.840
inflation. In other words, they were ok with&nbsp;
letting inflation run hot for a year or two as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:47.920
long as their policies boosted the labor market.&nbsp;
It wasn’t until the unemployment rate cratered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:53.200
sub 4% in late 2021 that inflation became their&nbsp;
number 1 priority. Now that they’re focusing on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:57.520
inflation, I don’t think they’ll go easy on&nbsp;
it. I mean, they didn’t hesitate to go from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.600
quarter percentage rate hikes to three quarter&nbsp;
percentage rate hikes in a matter of months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:07.280
So, I don’t think they’ll hesitate to go to 1%&nbsp;
or even higher either. As long as unemployment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.840
is low and inflation is high, they’ll pump&nbsp;
up interest rates as much as they need to.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.520
INFLATION OUTLOOK:&nbsp;

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:20.400
It’s great that the fed is finally taking serious&nbsp;
action to combat inflation, but how long will it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:26.160
take for inflation to actually cool down. Well,&nbsp;
there is no secret formula to calculate this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:30.560
but we can take a look at history to guide&nbsp;
our expectations. Over the past 100 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:35.200
we’ve seen 5 notable peaks of inflation.&nbsp;
4 out of these 5 times, inflation reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:41.600
levels of over 10% before peaking. But, in 1950,&nbsp;
inflation peaked at around the levels that we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.480
right now. From the peak, it took about 1 year&nbsp;
and 1 month for inflation to come back down to 2%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.800
Now of course, past performance is not indicative&nbsp;
of future results, but if we were to assume that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:58.000
something like that played out right now, we could&nbsp;
expect inflation cool down by August of 2023.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.680
But, that’s just the best case scenario. Inflation&nbsp;
could easily run to over 10% like it did 4 other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:09.520
times in history. In 1941, inflation continued&nbsp;
to rise for another 7 months after reaching 9%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:15.520
After this, it took another 21 months for&nbsp;
inflation to actually come back down. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:21.840
in 1974, inflation continued to rise for another&nbsp;
11 months after reaching 9%. And it took 23 months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:27.760
for inflation to cool back down. So, we could&nbsp;
easily see inflation continue to rise into Q2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:35.120
of 2023. From there, it could take till Q1 of 2025&nbsp;
for inflation to cool down to 3%. So, we’re by no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:40.720
means close to the end, but we may be close to the&nbsp;
peak. The fed seems to finally be taking inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:46.080
seriously, so chances are good that we see a peak&nbsp;
by the end of this year, but it’s still a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:51.680
way down. Most of 2023 would still be elevated&nbsp;
inflation even though it would be declining.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:57.600
And it wouldn’t be till 2024 or early 2025 that&nbsp;
we see normal levels. Even then, we wouldn’t be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.600
out of the woods though. Both times that we&nbsp;
saw high inflation in the past 100 years, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:08.720
saw multiple peaks. In the 1940s, we saw 3 peaks&nbsp;
and in the 1970s we saw 2 peaks. So, if inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:17.840
starts heading back up in 2025 or 2026, we’re&nbsp;
likely in for a full decade of high inflation.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:20.320
“TRANSITORY” INFLATION:
In the end, the fed expected inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:25.280
to be transitory due a recovering labor market&nbsp;
along with expectations for demand to cool down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:30.000
As as result, they were willing to let inflation&nbsp;
run hot for a little as the economy recovered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.960
But, the labor market would end up recovering&nbsp;
faster than they expected. Not to mention we also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:38.880
saw the supply shortage, the labor shortage,&nbsp;
and the Ukraine Russia war which screwed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:44.080
the Fed’s plan signficantly. At this point,&nbsp;
inflation has already rocketed to over 9%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:49.920
and all we can do is wait for the feds tightening&nbsp;
to take effect and for shortages to slowly ease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.080
Unfortunately, neither of these are fast&nbsp;
processes and we’re likely looking at a minimum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:58.960
of another year or two of elevated inflation&nbsp;
ahead of us. Even if things do cool down fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:03.120
we can’t let our guard down because history&nbsp;
has shown us that inflation often comes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:07.840
the form of multiple peaks. So, we would have to&nbsp;
continue staying cautious to avoid a second or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:13.360
even third peak. But, that’s just what I think.&nbsp;
When do you guys think inflation will peak?&nbsp;&nbsp;

